2026-11-11T23:04:43.045-08:00 [reviewer] RECEIVED topic="s0036_drafts" correlation_id="e1a931b0-8a15-45a4-990b-5cd29d35d5bc" partition=0 offset=276 kind="draft"
2026-11-11T23:04:43.053-08:00 [reviewer] SENT topic="s0036_final" correlation_id="e1a931b0-8a15-45a4-990b-5cd29d35d5bc" status="approved" answer="Yes, golang.org/x/net 0.15.0 is affected by the HTTP/2 rapid reset denial of service vulnerability (CVE-2023-44487). This high-severity issue exists in all versions below 0.17.0 per the s0036_rel advisory VULN-0036-HW9-19. The fix was implemented in version 0.17.0, resolving the HTTP/2 rapid reset attack vector. You must upgrade to **golang.org/x/net 0.17.0 or later** to mitigate this risk. Do not upgrade to 0.16.0\u2014this version does not contain the fix. Always verify the exact version in your project's dependency management system (e.g., Go modules) to ensure compatibility with the patched code. The vulnerability allows attackers to trigger rapid resets in HTTP/2 connections, causing denial of service. Update promptly to maintain security. (149 words)\n\nSource record: VULN-0036-HW9-19 (s0036_rel id 20092): golang.org/x/net affected < 0.17.0, severity high. CVE-2023-44487: HTTP/2 rapid reset denial of service. Fixed in 0.17.0."
2026-11-11T23:04:43.072-08:00 [reviewer] COMMITTED offset after processing correlation_id="e1a931b0-8a15-45a4-990b-5cd29d35d5bc" partition=0 offset=276
2026-11-11T23:07:53.766-08:00 [reviewer] RECEIVED topic="s0036_drafts" correlation_id="2b58fed7-649f-453d-bff4-1d5b53eb3aff" partition=0 offset=277 kind="draft"
2026-11-11T23:07:53.769-08:00 [reviewer] SENT topic="s0036_final" correlation_id="2b58fed7-649f-453d-bff4-1d5b53eb3aff" status="approved" answer="Generating a software bill of materials (SBOM) for every release is critical because it provides a transparent, machine-readable inventory of all components in a software stack. This enables teams to **identify vulnerabilities in dependencies** by cross-referencing components against known vulnerability databases (e.g., NVD), proactively uncovering risks before they exploit. It also **ensures regulatory compliance** with frameworks like NIST SP 800-161 and ISO 27001, which mandate traceability for security and supply chain integrity\u2014preventing audit failures and legal exposure. Finally, SBOMs **accelerate patching cycles** by precisely pinpointing affected components, reducing remediation time from days to hours. Without this visibility, teams risk undetected critical flaws, non-compliance penalties, and extended downtime during security incidents. In essence, SBOMs transform vulnerability management from reactive to proactive, directly supporting security resilience, regulatory adherence, and operational efficiency in every release cycle. (149 words)"
